# T02-W01 fixed-array comparison with actual element laws

The [ordinary component](../../../../../../crates/mpk-vc/src/go_practical_ordinary/array/equality.rs)
adds fixed-array equality and inequality at every frozen positive depth0..8,
plus the canonical empty representation. Its2,981 declarations/89,121 terms/
1,132,748 bytes preserve all2,688 prior identities and the complete1,033,685-byte
array producer. There are47 new definitions and246 ordinary laws, without an
axiom, function extensionality, core rule, caller-selected root or limit change.

For positive arrays, each visible slot contributes the actual element comparison;
padding contributes true. A balanced conjunction computes equality, and Boolean
negation computes inequality. Reflection and completeness lift actual typed
element-comparator laws and validity witnesses to the complete visible relation.
Inequality yields a real refutation of that relation; the converse consumes the
forward comparator law. Full64-bit read reflection also requires the actual shape
and range witnesses. Reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity require their real
conditional element laws. Pointwise congruence, normalization of both
operands, copy and relation-respecting comparator preservation use checked
ordinary proofs without equality of arbitrary functions.

The concrete byte instantiation fixes the existing full-eight-bit comparator and
its reflection/completeness proofs. Callers cannot supply its comparator or truth
values. A direct ByteNotEqualStep law binds the concrete negative wrapper to the
negation of concrete equality at every depth. Empty comparison returns true/false
for equality/inequality; canonical dummy equality still requires both validity
witnesses. These are mathematical components. Generic comparator parameters do
not admit Go callbacks or caller semantic adapters.

The [verification receipt](verification.json) records five affected Rust roots,
strict target Clippy and eleven-file format on identical current production/test
bytes. The [two-core receipt](paired-complete/result.json) checks783 complete
same-byte inputs:two zero-axiom acceptances and781 genuine type mismatches,
comprising246 changed conclusions,488 actual typed witness domains and47
typed wrong definitions. Every wrong runtime definition has an actual result
countermodel. Exact ownership rejects an otherwise core-accepted extension and
the unchanged byte cap. No unrelated whole suite or intermediate T gate runs.

The [native Go observation](native-go/result.json) compiles actual array `==` and
`!=` at all257 lengths0..256 using pinned Go1.25.0 darwin/arm64. It keeps1,028
whole256-byte input pairs, four variants per length, including visible first/last
and high-bit differences and differences only outside the logical length. The
unchanged finite interpreter evaluates the actual decoded ordinary definitions
for1,292 raw/normalized case pairs and2,584 operator results, all matching Go in
536,538,673 AST steps. Full original inputs and all64 length bits precede each
evaluation; no definition callback or theorem body supplies outputs. Existing
per-case50M-step/2M-value/2M-environment ceilings remain unchanged.

Native negative cases reject slice/map/function elements, distinct named array
types, a struct containing a slice and zero-length arrays of noncomparable
elements. These eight compiler rejections establish observation fixtures; source
comparability must still be enforced by the eventual admitted-source rules.
In particular, length zero does not make a forbidden element type comparable.
